correct, because repeatedly dividing by 5 elegantly counts all multiples of 5 that contribute to trailing zeroes while completely avoiding integer overflow.

REVIEW MY CODE

What I did well: you applied the optimal math approach we discussed previously, correctly accumulating the result of integer division by 5 instead of using modulo. You also correctly recognized that calculating the actual factorial is a trap.

Is it correct: yes, it is fully correct and passes all test cases.

Logical mistakes, and any edge cases my code misses: there are no logical bugs. It perfectly handles the edge case of 0, where the loop immediately skips and returns 0.

Time and space complexity of MY code, each with a one-line reason:

Time complexity: O(log5 n) because you divide n by 5 in every step, meaning the loop runs very few times even for massive inputs.

Space complexity: O(1) auxiliary space because you only use a single integer counter variable.

Why my approach works: trailing zeroes are created by multiplying 10, which is made of the prime factors 2 and 5. There are always more 2s than 5s in a factorial sequence, so we just need to count the 5s. Your n / 5 elegantly counts all multiples of 5 up to n, and repeating the loop counts the extra 5s in multiples of 25, then 125, and so on.

ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED
Approach 1: Compute and Count (Brute Force)

Idea: calculate the actual factorial of n, then repeatedly divide the result by 10 to count how many zeroes are at the end.

C++ Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    int trailingZeroes(int n) {
        unsigned long long fact = 1;
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
            fact *= i;
        }
        int count = 0;
        while (fact > 0 && fact % 10 == 0) {
            count++;
            fact /= 10;
        }
        return count;
    }
};
Time complexity: O(n) to compute the factorial sequentially.

Space complexity: O(1) auxiliary space.

What makes the next approach better: factorials grow incredibly fast. 13! already exceeds a standard 32-bit integer, and 21! exceeds a 64-bit integer, causing fatal integer overflow crashes for most inputs.

Approach 2: Prime Factor Counting (Optimal Math)

Idea: instead of computing the massive factorial, just count how many pairs of 2 and 5 exist in the numbers from 1 to n. Since 5s are rarer than 2s, just count the 5s by dividing n by 5, then 25, then 125, adding the quotients together.

C++ Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    int trailingZeroes(int n) {
        int count = 0;
        while (n > 0) {
            count += n / 5;
            n /= 5;
        }
        return count;
    }
};
Time complexity: O(log n) specifically base 5.

Space complexity: O(1) auxiliary space.

What makes the next approach better: this is the mathematical ceiling for this problem. It is the best possible approach.

WHERE I STAND

Which approach did I use: you used Approach 2, the optimal math approach.

Which approach does an interviewer expect for this problem: interviewers expect exactly this approach. They want to see that you understand prime factorization and recognize that computing the factorial directly is impossible due to memory limits.

Common follow-up questions interviewers ask on this problem, and how to answer them:

Question 1: Why do we only count 5s and not 2s?

Answer: a trailing zero requires multiplying by 10, which requires one factor of 2 and one factor of 5. In the sequence of numbers from 1 to n, every second number is even (giving a 2), but only every fifth number gives a 5. Because 5s are the limiting factor, counting 5s gives the exact number of pairs.

Question 2: Why do we need the while loop instead of just returning n / 5?

Answer: returning n / 5 only counts the numbers that have at least one factor of 5. However, numbers like 25 (5 * 5) contribute two 5s, and 125 (5 * 5 * 5) contribute three. The loop ensures we count those extra overlapping factors of 5.

WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBER (for my revision notes)

The key insight, in one sentence: a trailing zero is formed by multiplying 2 and 5, and since factorials have plenty of 2s, you only need to count the total factors of 5 by repeatedly dividing by 5.

My mistakes, as a short checklist for next time: your code is flawless this time. Just remember the difference between using integer division (n / 5) to count multiples across a sequence, versus modulo (n % 5) which only checks the specific number.

The pattern or idea this question is really about: prime factorization and avoiding integer overflow in combinatorial math problems.

